; 45-builtin.kurt

; `builtin` gives a symbol a meaning that Kurt itself implements -- nothing has
; such a meaning by its name alone.  Two kinds:
;
; - an operation of the calculator (40-calc.kurt): `builtin + add` makes `+`
;   the calculator's addition, computed with `calc on`
; - a role of the engine: prop.kurt says `builtin iff equivalence, or
;   disjunction, not negation, false falsum`, equality.kurt `builtin =
;   equality` -- so an `iff` fact is used as two implications, `case` splits an
;   `or`, an `assume` block ending in `false` gives `not`, and `def` defines
;   with `=` or `iff`
;
; Without arguments, `builtin` lists the meanings given so far.

load prop
builtin

; Only a theory that comes with Kurt (or one from a `-p` directory) can give a
; role of the engine, since it changes what Kurt derives.  Our own `<=>`
; stays an ordinary relation:

infix <=> 10 10
bool <=> 0 1 2
expect "EvalError" "can only be given by a theory that comes with Kurt"
    builtin <=> equivalence

; A calculator operation, like an axiom, can be given here too (not with
; `kurt --strict`): `plus` adds.

load equality
infix plus 30 30
builtin plus add
calc on
2 plus 3 = 5
